course: myths
lesson: 21
title: Ancient times and famous stories
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Isaac is sure the pyramids were built by whipped slaves, Leila knows thumbs down meant death in the arena, and Lucy's cartoon shows cavemen riding dinosaurs. Films and cartoons have painted the past in bright, wrong colours.
explain: Archaeologists (people who dig up and study old remains) dig up the real clues: bread ovens and workers' graves beside the pyramids, flakes of old paint on Greek statues, Stone Age huts in open fields, and court records from Salem.
explain: Ancient Greek statues, for example, were painted in bright reds, blues and golds; the paint wore off over two thousand years, and later people copied the bare white stone and thought that was the look.
explain: Some old stories are half true: Troy was a real city, but the wooden horse is a legend; frogs really were dropped into milk in some villages, but nobody proved it kept the milk fresh.
explain: When a mate says "everyone knows", ask "what did they dig up?" and "who first told it?"; and if they turn out to be half right, say so.
fact: Evidence near the Giza pyramids, such as workers' villages, bakeries and their own tombs, suggests they were mostly built by paid and fed Egyptian workers, some doing a duty to the king; historians still discuss the details.
fact: Many gladiators were expensive trained fighters, and many fights did not end in death; nobody knows exactly which thumb signal the Romans used, and "thumbs down means kill" was made famous by a painting from 1872.
fact: A Roman vomitorium was a wide passage that let crowds pour out of a stadium or theatre, not a room for being sick.
fact: Ancient Greek and Roman statues were often painted in bright colours; scientists find traces of the paint with special lights and microscopes.
fact: Troy was a real city in what is now Turkey, but there is no evidence for the wooden horse, which comes from ancient poems and legends.
fact: Stonehenge was begun about 5,000 years ago, more than 2,000 years before the Druids (priests of the ancient Celts) are first mentioned in history.
fact: Stone Age people mostly lived in huts, tents and camps in the open; caves were used for shelter at times and for paintings.
fact: In the Salem witch trials of 1692, nineteen people were hanged and one man was crushed to death with stones; nobody was burned there, though witches were burned in some other countries.
fact: Paul Revere's ride was meant to be secret, and the colonists (people living in Britain's lands in America) then thought of themselves as British; he is reported to have warned that "the Regulars are coming out", meaning the British army soldiers.
fact: The famous "The British are coming!" version comes mostly from a poem written in 1860, long after the ride.
fact: The last big dinosaurs died out about 66 million years ago, and humans appeared only about 300,000 years ago.
fact: In some Russian and Finnish villages before fridges, people are reported to have dropped a live frog into milk; scientists later found germ-fighting substances in the skin of one kind of frog, but nobody has shown it kept milk fresh, and it is not a safe idea.
fact: A film, a painting or a poem made long after the event is a story, not evidence.
exercise: Who mostly built the Giza pyramids, according to the evidence? | Paid and fed Egyptian workers, some doing a duty to the king | Whipped slaves captured from faraway lands | Giant machines left behind by visitors from space | Roman soldiers on holiday in Egypt
exercise: What evidence was found near the pyramids? | Workers' villages, bakeries and workers' tombs | Chains and whips found in every single room inside | Plans drawn on paper by a Roman engineer | Nothing at all except sand
exercise: Did gladiators always fight to the death? | No; many were costly trained fighters and many fights ended without death | Yes, every fight had to end with one fighter dead | Yes, and the winner also had to fight a lion | No, they only ever pretended to fight, like actors putting on a play for fun
exercise: Where does the idea "thumbs down meant kill" come mostly from? | A famous painting from 1872 | A Roman law written on a stone | A gladiator's diary found in Rome | The rules of the first Olympic Games
exercise: What was a Roman vomitorium? | A wide passage that let crowds pour out of a stadium | A room where guests went to be sick during big Roman feasts | A kitchen where the feast food was cooked | A small bath for washing after dinner
exercise: What did ancient Greek statues look like when new? | Often brightly painted | Always pure white marble | Covered in shiny gold leaf all over | Plain grey because paint did not exist
exercise: Which part of the Troy story is supported by evidence? | Troy was a real city | The wooden horse was real | The horse was found buried in the sand | Both the city and the horse were real
exercise: Who built Stonehenge? | People who lived long before the Druids | The Druids, as a temple for their sun festivals | The Romans, as a fort to guard their roads | Medieval monks, as a giant calendar for Easter
exercise: Where did Stone Age people mostly live? | In huts, tents and camps in the open | Only deep inside caves, all their lives | In stone castles with tall towers | In tree houses high above the ground
exercise: How were the people condemned in the Salem witch trials killed? | Nineteen were hanged and one man was crushed with stones | They were all burned on a big fire | They were all sent away on a ship | Nobody died, they were only fined
exercise: What is Paul Revere reported to have warned? | "The Regulars are coming out" | "The British are coming!" shouted loudly | "The French are coming by sea!" | "The king has arrived in Boston!"
exercise: Where does the "The British are coming!" version mostly come from? | A poem written in 1860 | Paul Revere's own diary | A newspaper of that very night | A song sung by his horse riders
exercise: How long ago did the last big dinosaurs die out? | About 66 million years ago | About 10,000 years ago | About 300,000 years ago | About 2,000 years ago
exercise: Ezra says medieval people put frogs in milk. What is closer to the truth? | Some villages are reported to have done it; nobody has shown it worked | It is a total lie that nobody ever did | It is proven to keep milk fresh for months | Every family in the Middle Ages did it every day
exercise: Is a film made long after an event good evidence for it? | No, it is a story, not evidence | Yes, films always check every fact | Yes, if the film is very old | Only if the actors are famous
test: "pyramids = slaves getting whipped, i saw it in a film" What would you tell your mate? | Digs suggest mostly paid, fed workers; films add the whips | The film is right; we even have old photos of the whipping | Aliens built them, so there were no workers | Romans built them with slaves from Britain
test: A video game shows every gladiator fight ending in death. Why would a real owner avoid that? | Trained gladiators were expensive, so losing one cost a lot | Gladiators were not allowed to touch each other | The crowd always wanted both fighters to walk out alive and well | Romans did not know how to use swords
test: Owen says Romans had vomitoriums for being sick at feasts. What was a vomitorium for? | Letting crowds pour out of a stadium | Storing leftover food after feasts | Training gladiators before fights | Keeping lions under the arena
test: Ava's art book shows a white Greek statue. What would it have looked like long ago? | Painted in bright colours that later wore off | Exactly the same as now, bare and white all over | Covered in mud to protect it | Painted black all over
test: "The Trojan Horse 100% happened, it's in a really old poem" What's the fairest reply? | Troy was real, but the horse is a legend with no evidence | It happened, because old poems like that one are always true | Troy never existed at all | The horse was real but Troy was made up
test: Grace says Druids built Stonehenge. What does the timing show? | It was begun over 2,000 years before Druids are mentioned | The Druids finished building it just before the Romans came | The Druids built it in the Middle Ages | The Druids and Stonehenge appeared on the same day
test: Lucy's cartoon shows a caveman riding a T. rex. What is wrong? | Dinosaurs died out about 66 million years before humans | Cavemen rode the smaller dinosaurs, just never the big T. rex | T. rex lived in caves with humans | Nothing, cartoons are always correct
test: Bella says witches were burned at Salem. How could she put it right? | People were hanged there; burning happened in other places | Nobody was ever accused of witchcraft at Salem, not even once | They were burned at Salem every single year | Salem is a made-up town from a film
test: "ur wrong ezra, frogs in milk is just a dumb story" Who is fairer? | Ezra is partly right; it's reported in some villages, but not proven to work | The friend: nobody ever put frogs in milk | Ezra: it is proven to keep milk fresh | Both are wrong, because frogs live only in milk
test: Your friend thinks all Stone Age people lived in caves. What could you show them? | Remains of huts and camps found in the open | A cartoon of a family in a cave | A modern house with stone walls | A photograph of a cave taken during the Stone Age
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the appearance of ancient Greek statuary at the time of its creation. | It was frequently painted in vivid colours | It was invariably left as unpainted white marble | It was uniformly gilded with gold leaf | It was exclusively painted black
exam: Identify the function properly ascribed to the Roman structure designated a vomitorium. | A passage permitting the rapid exit of crowds | A chamber reserved for illness during banquets | A kitchen for the preparation of feasts | A cell for the confinement of animals
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the chronological relationship between humans and the large dinosaurs? | The large dinosaurs became extinct approximately sixty-six million years before humans appeared | Humans and large dinosaurs coexisted for several thousand years | Humans appeared shortly before the extinction of the large dinosaurs | The large dinosaurs became extinct approximately two thousand years ago
exam: It is given that the persons condemned at Salem in the year 1692 were put to death. Ascertain the manner in which nineteen of them were executed. | By hanging | By burning | By drowning | By exile at sea
